---
import { getCollection } from "astro:content";
import GuideList from "../../components/GuideList.astro";
import Simple from "../../layouts/Simple.astro";

const articles = (await getCollection("articles", ({ data }) => data.section === "guides")).sort(
  (a, b) => a.data.order - b.data.order,
);
---
<Simple
  title="Analytics guides"
  description="Practical guides to marketing attribution, cookieless analytics, the DataFast API, and DataFast MCP, written for founders choosing a stack."
  kicker="Guides"
  lede="These guides explain a decision, then compare the tools that can carry it. They are not a single ranked list of logos."
  crumbs={[{ href: "/", label: "Home" }, { label: "Guides" }]}
>
  <p>Attribution is the question of which visit should get credit for a payment. Cookieless analytics is the question of how stable that credit can be if you refuse a long-lived browser identifier. Those two guides answer them separately, because a cookieless script and a revenue report pull in opposite directions once a sale takes weeks instead of minutes.</p>
  <p>Two DataFast guides sit next to those decisions. The <a href="/guides/datafast-api/">DataFast API</a> page maps the official docs: website keys, goal events, revenue, the CLI, and where MCP fits. The <a href="/guides/datafast-mcp/">DataFast MCP</a> guide is the assistant connection for an account you already have at datafa.st. Neither one is a pricing page or a review. The <a href="/reviews/datafast/">DataFast review</a>, the <a href="/pricing/datafast/">DataFast pricing</a> ladder, the <a href="/alternatives/datafast/">DataFast alternatives</a>, and the <a href="/compare/datafast-vs-plausible/">DataFast vs Plausible</a> comparison are the neighboring pages. API and MCP steps were read from DataFast’s docs on 25 November 2026. The attribution and cookieless guides cite vendor pages checked on 24 November 2026 unless a sentence says otherwise.</p>
  <section slot="wide" class="band band-yellow">
    <div class="article-wide">
      <h2>Guides</h2>
      <GuideList
        items={articles.map((article) => ({
          href: `/${article.id}/`,
          title: article.data.title,
          description: article.data.description,
        }))}
      />
    </div>
  </section>
</Simple>
